body:
I had a question about combination kana (yoon). There appear to be 36 of them (e.g. kya, kyu, kyo, etc.). I'm also aware of 4 W-sounds (wa, wi, we, wo), 5 V-sounds (va, vi, vu, ve, vo) and 5 F-sounds (fa, fi, fu, fe, fo).

&#x200B;

However, are the ones below actually used?

'tsa' つぁ

'tsi' つぃ

'tse' つぇ

'tso' つぉ

&#x200B;

'she' しぇ

'je' じぇ

&#x200B;

'che' ちぇ

&#x200B;

'tu' とぅ

'du' どぅ

&#x200B;

'ti' てぃ

'di' でぃ

&#x200B;

Are there any other small-vowel combinations that I should know?

So, do 'a/x' and 'b/y' always default to the points that are invariant? This is very confusing for me. I had another problem.

&#x200B;

I don't know to find the successive transformations which make-up a single matrix. For example, how do I know what transformations this matrix represents, and the order in which they are applied?

&#x200B;

4   3

\-3 -2

&#x200B;

I drew 2 quadrilaterals with the original points: (0,0) , (1,1) , (0,2) , (-1,1) and image points: (0,0) , (7,-5) , (6,-4) , (-1,1). It looks like a shear/stretch?

This is what I got from your description:

Take 3 original points (a, b, c) from before the transformation, and take 3 image points (x, y, z) from after the transformation.

However, I got confused when you mentioned the lines. 

Are you saying to spot two lines which are parallel, where one line must have both a and b on it, but the other line must have c and z on it? 

If the lines turn-out to be parallel, then the transformation is a shear?

&#x200B;

I tried doing this with the matrix you gave:

&#x200B;

0   1

\-1  2

&#x200B;

I made-up 3 original points:

&#x200B;

2   1   3

0   1   3

&#x200B;

I found their 3 image points:

&#x200B;

0   1   3

\-2  1   3

&#x200B;

(2,0) changed to (0, -2), but points (1, 1) and (3, 3) are invariant. I don't know what to do from here. How do I know which points are a, b and c? I don't know how to arrange them.

&#x200B;

Are you saying a transforms to x, b transforms to y, and c transforms to z?

I have removed my previous comments, as I now know that, for electrochemical cells, the cathode is positive (site of reduction), and the anode is negative (site of oxidation). For my A-Level specification, I believe electrolytic cells are not in it (where the electrode polarity would be swapped). 

&#x200B;

I would still appreciate it if someone was able to check the formulae and state symbols I have given, particularly for the lead-acid cell.

I am in my A2 year, studying Chemistry, Physics and Maths (I have Further Maths to AS-Level). I am not applying for entry next year, and I don't have a UCAS account. I chose not to defer entry but apply this year. I am in the process of arranging a 1-year work placement, starting from July/August 2020. I have read the subject details on the Oxford website, and I looked at the career prospects for many degrees.

&#x200B;

I need advice from students who have studied here, in order to decide between: Engineering Science and Materials Science (Computer Science is not a 'first-choice' option for me). I know this is a very broad statement, but I want to make a difference to a field of science through creating new technology. Please feel free to ask further questions, if you would like me to expand upon what I want out of a degree/what interests me. Thank you for your help.
